title: Masks: keeping some bits
mode: vhdl
chapter: Buses and numbers
check: behaves
---- intro
An AND on a bus is a **mask**: where the mask bit is 1, the data bit goes through; where it is 0, the result is 0.

```
a     1 0 1 1
m     0 0 1 1
a and m  0 0 1 1
```

Programs use masks all the time, to pick out the few bits of a number they care about.
---- task
`y` is the bits of `a` that `m` lets through: 4 AND gates in one line.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity mask4 is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    m : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    y : out std_logic_vector(3 downto 0)
  );
end mask4;

architecture rtl of mask4 is
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity mask4 is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    m : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    y : out std_logic_vector(3 downto 0)
  );
end mask4;

architecture rtl of mask4 is
begin
    y <= a and m;
end rtl;
---- hint1
It looks exactly like a one-bit AND.
---- hint2
`y <= a and m;`
---- solution
`y <= a and m;` Set `m` to `"1100"` and only the top two bits of `a` come through.
---- done
OR with a mask sets bits to 1, AND with a mask clears them, XOR with a mask flips them. That is most of what low-level code does to hardware registers.
